#################################################################
#    Markdown explanations
#################################################################

<https://ekgf.org/ontology/story#>
    annotation:markdownDescription """
# Stories and concept usage

A story describes an interaction within a use case, including the persona
playing it, the outcome it contributes to, and the concepts it uses. Describe
these as **input concepts**, **output concepts**, and **dependent concepts**.
An input concept is part of the business requirement; its technical
manifestations can include API names, query variables, or source-code
identifiers.

## Implementations and ownership

`implementedBy` links a story to its implementations. Its inverse,
`implementationOf`, identifies the single story an implementation belongs to.
A story can have zero or more implementations. Each implementation belongs to
exactly one story.

The OWL class restriction records exactly one owner, and `implementationOf`
is functional. OWL can equate different owner names when their distinctness
has not been asserted. `StoryImplementationShape` validates the supplied data
and rejects missing or multiple owner terms, counting both link directions.
An owner asserted in both directions is counted once. The shape also covers
implementation subclasses and relationship endpoints whose types are inferred.

The number of implementations does not select which one executes. A Story
Service resolves an appropriate runnable implementation in its execution
context and rejects invocation when that selection is absent or ambiguous.

### Migration and validation

Replace every `hasImplementation` assertion with `implementedBy`, or use
`implementationOf` in the other direction. `LegacyImplementationShape`
rejects the removed predicate, including when a canonical link is also present.
Replace previously loaded ontology graphs so the old declaration and inverse
axiom are removed.

For a Turtle data file, validate with the Story Ontology as both the shapes
graph and the ontology graph. RDFS inference supplies endpoint types and
subclass relationships:

```sh
pyshacl -s ekgf-story.ttl -e ekgf-story.ttl -i rdfs implementation-data.ttl
```

Either direction can be supplied:

```turtle
@prefix ekg-story: <https://ekgf.org/ontology/story#> .
@prefix ex: <https://example.org/> .

ex:findCustomer ekg-story:implementedBy ex:findCustomerSPARQL .
# Equivalently:
# ex:findCustomerSPARQL ekg-story:implementationOf ex:findCustomer .
```

Validate the graph or deliberately selected graph set representing one
coherent version context. Historical snapshots can repeat the same story
and implementation IRIs without adding owners. If snapshots link the same
implementation IRI to different story-version IRIs, each snapshot must be
validated separately; combining them produces a multiple-owner violation.
Named graphs alone do not define this context or exempt ownership constraints.

## Ownership and reuse

A story owns its concept-usage objects. Each usage belongs to exactly one
story and references exactly one reusable `ekg-concept:DataElementConcept`
through `ekg-concept:hasConcept`. Different stories reference the same concept
through separate usage objects. Deleting a story removes its usage objects;
the referenced concepts retain their identity.

Concepts are members of `ekg-concept:ConceptSet` resources. A use case owns its
concept vocabularies through `ekg-use-case:ownsConceptSet`; stories can draw
from their use case's vocabularies and those inherited from ancestor use cases.
Using a concept does not transfer its ownership to the story.

## Agreed replacement model

**Implementation status:** this decision is recorded here; the current axioms
still contain earlier generations and require the migration described below.

| Story predicate | Range of the predicate |
| --- | --- |
| `hasInputConcept` | `StoryInput` |
| `hasOutputConcept` | `StoryOutput` |
| `hasDependentConcept` | `StoryDependent` |

The three range classes specialize a common `StoryConceptUsage` class. The
three predicates specialize `hasConceptUsage`. A specific predicate lets the
reasoner infer both the specific usage class and its common superclass, and
the general relationship. A separate usage-type value is unnecessary.
These usage classes describe relationships to concepts.

For example, the target model allows:

```turtle
@prefix ekg-story: <https://ekgf.org/ontology/story#> .
@prefix ekg-concept: <https://ekgf.org/ontology/concept#> .
@prefix ex: <https://example.org/> .

ex:findCustomer ekg-story:hasInputConcept [
    ekg-concept:hasConcept ex:customerIdentifier ;
    ekg-story:isMandatory true
] .
```

The blank node is the story-owned input-concept usage. Its type is inferred
from the predicate's range. The customer-identifier concept can be reused by
other stories.

## Input-concept behavior

An input usage can carry `isMandatory` and `isServerProvided`.
`isMandatory` means that the input is required for execution;
`isServerProvided` means that the Story Service supplies it. The flags are
independent: an input can be mandatory and server-provided.

A Story Service interprets each absent flag as **false**. Missing flags remain
valid data. Planned SHACL property shapes record `sh:defaultValue false`,
accept at most one value per flag, and require supplied values to be
`xsd:boolean`. Invalid values are validation errors.

`sh:defaultValue` records the application default; SHACL Core validation does
not insert it. OWL reasoning does not infer false from absence. A service
applies these defaults during execution; materializing explicit false triples
is an application choice.

## Migration status

Currently `hasInput` and `hasOutput` point to usage objects, while
`hasInputConcept`, `hasOutputConcept`, and `hasDependentConcept` point
directly to data element concepts. The replacement changes the latter
predicates' ranges to the usage classes, migrates their assertions, and retires
the competing `hasInput` and `hasOutput` predicates.

`StoryConceptUsage`, `StoryDependent`, `hasConceptUsage`, the two input flags,
and the corresponding SHACL shapes are not yet declared in this source.
Existing `MandatoryParameter`, `OptionalParameter`, `OptionalStoryOutput`,
and `serverProvidedIRI` declarations also require reconciliation with the
agreed usage model. The existing domain restriction on
`ekg-concept:hasConcept` must cover all three usage classes.

See the [Method's Story/Concept Relationship](https://method.ekgf.org/concept/concept/story-concept-relationship/#ontology),
its [concept-vocabulary ownership rules](https://method.ekgf.org/concept/concept/concept-vocabulary/#ontology),
and [SHACL default-value semantics](https://www.w3.org/TR/shacl/#defaultValue).
"""@en .

:StoryInput annotation:markdownDescription """
## A story's input-concept usage

This resource describes how one story uses a reusable concept as an input.
The story owns this usage object; `ekg-concept:hasConcept` references the shared
data element concept. Separate stories use separate usage objects even when
they reference the same concept.

The agreed replacement model makes this class a subclass of
`StoryConceptUsage` and the range of `hasInputConcept`. Input characteristics
such as requiredness and server provision belong on the usage. Their agreed
false defaults are applied by the Story Service.

Those superclass, range, flag, and SHACL changes are pending. See the ontology's
Markdown description for the decision and migration status.
"""@en .

:StoryOutput annotation:markdownDescription """
## A story's output-concept usage

This resource describes how one story uses a reusable concept as an output.
The story owns the usage object; `ekg-concept:hasConcept` references the shared
data element concept. A concept can describe a compound output, including the
meaning represented by a whole class or shape.

The agreed replacement model makes this class a subclass of
`StoryConceptUsage` and the range of `hasOutputConcept`. Those axiom changes
are pending; the current `hasOutputConcept` range is still
`ekg-concept:DataElementConcept`.
"""@en .

:StoryImplementation annotation:markdownDescription """
## One owning story

Each implementation belongs to exactly one story through `implementationOf`.
The inverse `implementedBy` lets a story refer to zero or more implementations.
An implementation can evolve independently of its story and coexist with other
implementations of that story.

The OWL cardinality restriction expresses ownership. SHACL validates actual
owner assertions in either direction, rejecting missing owners and multiple
owner terms. This validation is required because OWL's open-world semantics
does not report a missing assertion as a data error, and functionality can
equate owner names.

Validate within one coherent version context. Repeated assertions about the
same story identity across snapshots still identify one owner. Different
story-version IRIs count as different owner terms when their graphs are
combined; historical snapshots must be validated in their intended contexts.

The ontology's Markdown description includes the validation command and
migration from `hasImplementation` to the canonical inverse pair.
"""@en .

:implementedBy annotation:markdownDescription """
## Story to implementation

Links a story to each of its implementations. Its inverse is
`implementationOf`; asserting either direction lets an OWL reasoner derive
the other. Several implementations can belong to the same story.

Execution selection belongs to the Story Service's execution context. This
relationship records ownership of implementations. The retired
`hasImplementation` predicate must be migrated to this predicate.
"""@en .

:implementationOf annotation:markdownDescription """
## Implementation to its single story

Identifies the one story implemented by this resource. It is functional and
the inverse of `implementedBy`. `StoryImplementationShape` counts owners
from both directions, so duplicating an assertion in its inverse form still
identifies one owner, while conflicting links are rejected.
"""@en .

#################################################################
#    Validation shapeset
#################################################################

:ValidationShapeSet a owl:NamedIndividual , ekg-shape:ShapeSet ;
    rdfs:label "Story Validation Shapes"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Validates single-story implementation ownership and rejects retired implementation links."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://ekgf.org/ontology/story#> ;
    ekg-shape:shapePurpose ekg-shape:Validation ;
    ekg-shape:hasShape :StoryImplementationShape ,
                       :LegacyImplementationShape .
